    "result_text": "Build succeeds and all tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the daily owner-registration cap: creating an owner is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100 or more owners already exist for that day (by `registrationDate`).\n\nI followed the existing per-city-capacity rule as the template, keeping each piece of logic in its natural layer rather than concentrating it in the controller:\n\n1. **`DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`** (new, in `rest.validation`) \u2014 mirrors `CityCapacityExceededException`, carrying the offending `registrationDate`.\n\n2. **Repository layer** \u2014 added `countByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)` to the `OwnerRepository` interface. For the active `spring-data-jpa` profile it's derived automatically from the method name (like `countByCity`); I also added concrete implementations to the JPA (`JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl`) and JDBC (`JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl`, using the `registration_date` column) profiles so the whole codebase still compiles.\n\n3. **Service layer** \u2014 added `countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate)` to `ClinicService` and a thin `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` delegate in `ClinicServiceImpl`.\n\n4. **Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`) \u2014 added `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` and a check placed alongside the city-capacity check. It computes the effective registration date as `owner.getRegistrationDate()` when supplied, else `LocalDate.now()` (matching the entity's `@PrePersist` default), and throws the new exception when the day's count is already \u2265 100.\n\n5. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` mapping the exception to `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`, producing the same RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` shape as the other rules with `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]`.\n\n**Tests:** `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` passes, and the full `./mvnw test` suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
